"Zero-Temperature Phase Transitions of Antiferromagnetic Ising Model of General Spin on a Triangular Lattice (https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/9609007)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 30.1 years ago; We map the ground-state ensemble of antiferromagnetic Ising model of spin-S on a triangular lattice to an interface model whose entropic fluctuations are proposed to be described by an effective Gaussian free energy, which enables us to calculate the critical exponents of various operators in terms of the stiffness constant of the interface. ... The phase diagram at finite temperatures is also discussed.\nTitle: Zero-Temperature Phase Transitions of Antiferromagnetic Ising Model of General Spin on a Triangular Lattice\nAuthors: C. Zeng, C. L. Henley\nDate: Sat Aug 31 20:19:58 1996\n\nWe map the ground-state ensemble of antiferromagnetic Ising model of spin-S on a triangular lattice to an interface model whose entropic fluctuations are proposed to be described by an effective Gaussian free energy, which enables us to calculate the critical exponents of various operators in terms of the stiffness constant of the interface. Monte Carlo simulations for the ground-state ensemble utilizing this interfacial representation are performed to study both the dynamical and the static properties of the model. This method yields more accurate numerical results for the critical exponents. By varying the spin magnitude in the model, we find that the model exhibits three phases with a Kosterlitz-Thouless phase transition at 3/2<S_{KT}<2 and a locking phase transition at 5/2 < S_L \\leq 3. The phase diagram at finite temperatures is also discussed.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniversality for the random-cluster model on isoradial graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.02338)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.9 years ago; We show that the canonical random-cluster measure associated to isoradial graphs is critical for all $q \\geq 1$. ... For $1 \\leq q \\leq 4$, the arm exponents (assuming their existence) are shown to be the same for all isoradial graphs.\nTitle: Universality for the random-cluster model on isoradial graphs\nAuthors: Hugo Duminil-Copin, Jhih-Huang Li, Ioan Manolescu\nDate: Tue Nov  7 08:49:25 2017\n\nWe show that the canonical random-cluster measure associated to isoradial graphs is critical for all $q \\geq 1$. Additionally, we prove that the phase transition of the model is of the same type on all isoradial graphs: continuous for $1 \\leq q \\leq 4$ and discontinuous for $q > 4$. For $1 \\leq q \\leq 4$, the arm exponents (assuming their existence) are shown to be the same for all isoradial graphs. In particular, these properties also hold on the triangular and hexagonal lattices. Our results also include the limiting case of quantum random-cluster models in $1+1$ dimensions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlternating Arm Exponents for the Critical Planar Ising Model (https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.00985)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.4 years ago; Title: Alternating Arm Exponents for the Critical Planar Ising Model\nTitle: Alternating Arm Exponents for the Critical Planar Ising Model\nAuthors: Hao Wu\nDate: Tue May  3 16:59:51 2016\n\nWe derive the alternating arm exponents of critical Ising model. We obtain six different patterns of alternating boundary arm exponents which correspond to the boundary conditions $(\\ominus\\oplus)$, $(\\ominus\\text{free})$ and $(\\text{free}\\text{free})$, and the alternating interior arm exponents.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBond percolation on isoradial graphs: criticality and universality (https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.0505)\nciteturn1academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.5 years ago; In an investigation of percolation on isoradial graphs, we prove the criticality of canonical bond percolation on isoradial embeddings of planar graphs, thus extending celebrated earlier results for homogeneous and inhomogeneous square, triangular, and other lattices. ... As a consequence, we obtain the universality of these models at the critical point, in the sense that the one-arm and 2j-alternating-arm critical exponents (and therefore also the connectivity and volume exponents) are constant across the family of such percolation processes.\nTitle: Bond percolation on isoradial graphs: criticality and universality\nAuthors: Geoffrey Grimmett, Ioan Manolescu\nDate: Mon Apr  2 19:36:42 2012\n\nIn an investigation of percolation on isoradial graphs, we prove the criticality of canonical bond percolation on isoradial embeddings of planar graphs, thus extending celebrated earlier results for homogeneous and inhomogeneous square, triangular, and other lattices. This is achieved via the star-triangle transformation, by transporting the box-crossing property across the family of isoradial graphs. As a consequence, we obtain the universality of these models at the critical point, in the sense that the one-arm and 2j-alternating-arm critical exponents (and therefore also the connectivity and volume exponents) are constant across the family of such percolation processes. The isoradial graphs in question are those that satisfy certain weak conditions on their embedding and on their track system. This class of graphs includes, for example, isoradial embeddings of periodic graphs, and graphs derived from rhombic Penrose tilings.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTricriticality and finite-size scaling in the triangular Blume-Capel ferromagnet | Searcharxiv (https://searcharxiv.com/abs/2411.11689)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; We report on numerical simulations of the two-dimensional spin-$1$ Blume-Capel ferromagnet embedded in a triangular lattice. ... Extensive multicanonical simulations identifying transition points across the phase diagram characterize the Ising universality class for $Δ< Δ_{\\rm t}$ with precise determination of thermal and magnetic critical exponents expected in the second-order regime.On the other hand, for $Δ> Δ_{\\rm t}$, a finite-size scaling analysis is dedicated to revealing the first-order signature in the surface tension that linearly increases upon lowering the temperature deeper into the first-order transition regime. ...     http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\n\narXiv · 2411.11689\n\n# Tricriticality and finite-size scaling in the triangular Blume-Capel ferromagnet\n\nDimitrios Mataragkas·Alexandros Vasilopoulos·Nikolaos G. Fytas·Dong-Hee Kim\n\nView original source ↗Read source document ↗Export metadata ↓\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe report on numerical simulations of the two-dimensional spin-$1$ Blume-Capel ferromagnet embedded in a triangular lattice. Utilizing a range of Monte Carlo and finite-size scaling techniques, we explore several critical aspects along the crystal field--temperature ($Δ, T$) transition line. Wang-Landau simulations measuring the joint density of states in combination with the method of field mixing allow us to probe the phase coexistence curve in high resolution, determining the tricritical point $(Δ_{\\rm t}, T_{\\rm t})$ with improved accuracy and verifying the tricritical exponents. Extensive multicanonical simulations identifying transition points across the phase diagram characterize the Ising universality class for $Δ< Δ_{\\rm t}$ with precise determination of thermal and magnetic critical exponents expected in the second-order regime. On the other hand, for $Δ> Δ_{\\rm t}$, a finite-size scaling analysis is dedicated to revealing the first-order signature in the surface tension that linearly increases upon lowering the temperature deeper into the first-order transition regime. Finally, a comprehensive picture of the phase diagram for the model is presented, collecting transition points obtained from the combined numerical approach in this study and previous estimates in the literature.\n\nRECORD DETAILS\n\nSource\n    arXiv\nPublication date\n    2025-01-08\nSource updated\n    2025-02-28\nDOI\n    10.1103/physrevresearch.7.013214\nReuse permissions\n    http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\n\nMetadata is provided by the original archive. This page does not imply that the source document or image is freely reusable.\n\nDimitrios Mataragkas, Alexandros Vasilopoulos, Nikolaos G. Fytas, Dong-Hee Kim. 2025-01-08. Tricriticality and finite-size scaling in the triangular Blume-Capel ferromagnet. https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevresearch.7.013214\n\nCite the original work for its findings. Save a collection to share your selection of sources.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZero-temperature phase transitions of an antiferromagnetic Ising model of general spin on a triangular lattice | Phys. Rev. B (https://journals.aps.org/prb/abstract/10.1103/PhysRevB.55.14935)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 29.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Zero-temperature phase transitions of an antiferromagnetic Ising model of general spin on a triangular lattice ... We map the ground-state ensemble of antiferromagnetic Ising model of spin-S on a triangular lattice to an interface model whose entropic fluctuations are proposed to be described by an effective Gaussian free energy, which enables us to calculate the critical exponents of various operators in terms of the stiffness constant of the interface. ... The phase diagram at finite temperatures is also discussed.\n\n# Zero-temperature phase transitions of an antiferromagnetic Ising model of general spin on a triangular lattice\n\nChen Zeng\n\nChristopher L. Henley\n\n  * ^{}Department of Physics, Syracuse University, Syracuse, New York 13210\n\n  * ^{}Department of Physics, Cornell University, Ithaca, New York 14853\n\nPDF\n\nPhys. Rev. B 55, 14935 – Published 1 June, 1997\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.55.14935\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe map the ground-state ensemble of antiferromagnetic Ising model of spin-S on a triangular lattice to an interface model whose entropic fluctuations are proposed to be described by an effective Gaussian free energy, which enables us to calculate the critical exponents of various operators in terms of the stiffness constant of the interface. Monte Carlo simulations for the ground-state ensemble utilizing this interfacial representation are performed to study both the dynamical and the static properties of the model. This method yields more accurate numerical results for the critical exponents. By varying the spin magnitude in the model, we find that the model exhibits three phases with a Kosterlitz-Thouless phase transition at <{\\mathrm{S}}_{\\mathrm{KT}}<2 and a locking phase transition at <{\\mathrm{S}}_{\\mathrm{L}}⩽3. The phase diagram at finite temperatures is also discussed.\n\nPhys. Rev. B 55, 14935– Published 1 June, 1997\n\nVol. 55, Iss. 22 — 1 June 1997\n\n[Button: Export Citation]\n\nReuse & Permissions\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.55.14935\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniversality for the random-cluster model on isoradial graphs - Science Explorer Abstract (https://scixplorer.org/abs/2017arXiv171102338D/abstract)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; We show that the canonical random-cluster measure associated to isoradial graphs is critical for all $q \\geq 1$. ... For $1 \\leq q \\leq 4$, the arm exponents (assuming their existence) are shown to be the same for all isoradial graphs. ... Publication  | eprint arXiv:1711.02338\n\nAbstract\n\nAbstract\n\n\n## Universality for the random-cluster model on isoradial graphs\n\n### Authors\n\nDuminil-Copin, Hugo;\n\nLi, Jhih-Huang;\n\nManolescu, Ioan\n\nEprint\n\nFull Text Sources\n\nOther Resources\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe show that the canonical random-cluster measure associated to isoradial graphs is critical for all $q \\geq 1$. Additionally, we prove that the phase transition of the model is of the same type on all isoradial graphs: continuous for $1 \\leq q \\leq 4$ and discontinuous for $q > 4$. For $1 \\leq q \\leq 4$, the arm exponents (assuming their existence) are shown to be the same for all isoradial graphs. In particular, these properties also hold on the triangular and hexagonal lattices. Our results also include the limiting case of quantum random-cluster models in $1+1$ dimensions.\n\n### Details\n\nPublication  | eprint arXiv:1711.02338\nPublication Date  | November 2017\nDOI  | 10.48550/arXiv.1711.02338\narXiv  | arXiv:1711.02338\nBibcode  | 2017arXiv171102338D\nCollections  | physics general\nKeywords  | Mathematics - Probability Mathematical Physics\nE-Print Comment(s)  | 69 pages, 33 figures; Electron. J. Probab. 23: 1-70 (2018); doi:10.1214/18-EJP223--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZero-temperature phase transitions of an antiferromagnetic Ising model of general spin on a triangular lattice (https://explore.openaire.eu/search/result?pid=10.1103%2Fphysrevb.55.14935)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 29.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; description Publication keyboard_double_arrow_right Article , Preprint 01 Jun 1997 Embargo end date: 01 Sep 1996 English Publisher:American Physical Society (APS)Journal:Physical Review B, volume 55, pages 14,935-14,947 (issn: 0163-1829, eissn: 1095-3795, Copyright policy )Funded by:NSF | Theoretical Studies of Fl..., NSF | Spin Order Due to Disorde... ... doi: 10.1103/physrevb.55.14935 , 10.48550/arxiv.cond-mat/9609007 ... We map the ground-state ensemble of antiferromagnetic Ising model of spin-S on a triangular lattice to an interface model whose entropic fluctuations are proposed to be described by an effective Gaussian free energy, whic